<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is necrotizing fasciitis of the face?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Necrotizing fasciitis is a rare but life-threatening bacterial infection that destroys the fascia (connective tissue) beneath the skin. On the face, it spreads extremely rapidly and causes severe pain disproportionate to clinical appearance, skin discoloration, blistering, and rapid systemic deterioration. It requires immediate aggressive surgical debridement alongside broad-spectrum IV antibiotics. Mortality is high without prompt treatment.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How is a deep space infection different from cellulitis?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Cellulitis affects the superficial skin layers and is treated with antibiotics alone. A deep space infection involves the anatomical spaces between facial muscles and fascia — it can trap pus in locations unreachable by oral antibiotics alone. Space infections often require surgical drainage and carry higher risk of airway compromise. CT imaging is typically needed to assess their full extent.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can facial cellulitis become life-threatening?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — while mild cellulitis is manageable as an outpatient, untreated or inadequately treated facial cellulitis can spread to involve deep spaces (causing airway obstruction), spread to the orbit (causing vision loss), or enter the bloodstream causing sepsis. Our surgeons at CARE Hospitals admit moderate-severe cases for IV antibiotics and monitoring.</div>
</details>
</div>
</div>
